# Which words came from the Vikings?
subtitle: "Tap the everyday English words that came from the Vikings' Old Norse, and hear what 'window' and 'husband' first meant."

## Which words came from the Vikings?
text: {"font_size":34}
sources:
    - id: oed
      title: 'Online Etymology Dictionary: window, husband, egg, sky, knife, they'
      authors: D. Harper
      year: 2024
      url: https://www.etymonline.com
      finding: window from Old Norse vindauga 'wind-eye'; husband from húsbóndi; egg, sky, knife and they from Old Norse
    - id: caxton
      title: Prologue to Eneydos
      authors: William Caxton
      year: 1490
      finding: a merchant asking for 'egges' in Kent was told the woman spoke no French; she called them 'eyren'

    card title "Which words came from the Vikings?"

Which of these everyday English words did the Vikings bring with them?
    cards words cites ["oed"] select "From Old Norse"
        * "window" in true
        * "beef" in false
        * "sky" in true
        * "table" in false
        * "egg" in true
        * "husband" in true
        * "chair" in false
        * "knife" in true

    ask "Which of these words came into English from the Vikings' Old Norse? Tap every one." on words store v feedback.style warm feedback.perfect "Spoken like a true Dane." feedback.good "Most of the raid was a success." feedback.poor "The Normans muddied the waters here." feedback.none "A tricky crossing. The French words hide well."
        right: All {v.total} right.
        wrong: {v} of {v.total} on the first try.

Window is Norse vindauga: a wind-eye, the hole in the roof that let the smoke out and the air in.
    text gl_window "vindauga = wind-eye" #2f6b8f x 500 y 265 font_size 44

    erase gl_window

Husband is húsbóndi, the one who keeps the house.
    text gl_husband "húsbóndi = house-keeper" #2f6b8f x 500 y 265 font_size 44

Sky, egg and knife came the same way, from Vikings who settled in northern and eastern England from the eight hundreds.
    highlight words_3 words_5 words_8

    erase gl_husband

For centuries, the old English word for eggs lived on beside it: eyren.
    text gl_egg "Old English: eyren" #2f6b8f x 500 y 265 font_size 44

In 1490 the printer William Caxton told of a merchant who asked a woman in Kent for eggs, and was told she spoke no French.
    point at.ref words_5 gesture underline

She only understood when someone else asked for eyren.
    highlight words_5

    erase gl_egg

    text gl_french "Norman French" #2f6b8f x 500 y 265 font_size 44

Beef and chair are French: they came with the Normans after 1066, and table, older, was reinforced by them.
    highlight words_2 words_4 words_7

    erase gl_french

And one of the commonest words in English is Norse as well: they.
    text gl_they "they = Norse þeir" #2f6b8f x 500 y 265 font_size 44
---
